{"name":"Arame Seaweed Green","hex":"#3f4635","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"arame","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A seaweed, Eisenia bicyclis, used in Japanese cuisine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/arame"},{"term":"seaweed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of numerous marine algae, such as a kelp.","Any of various fresh water plants and algae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seaweed"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Arame","blocks":[{"text":"Arame , sea oak is a species of kelp, of the brown algae, best known for its use in Japanese cuisine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Alger%2C_Ecklonia_bicyclis%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png/330px-Alger%2C_Ecklonia_bicyclis%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arame","wikidata":"Q2703932"},{"title":"Seaweed","blocks":[{"text":"Seaweed, or macroalgae, refers to thousands of species of macroscopic, multicellular, marine algae. The term includes some types of Rhodophyta (red), Phaeophyta (brown) and Chlorophyta (green) macroalgae. Seaweed species such as kelps provide essential nursery habitat for fisheries and other marine species and thus protect food sources; other species, such as planktonic algae, play a vital role in capturing carbon and producing at least 50% of Earth's oxygen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Fucus_serratus_2015-09-08_ag_M0010140.jpg/330px-Fucus_serratus_2015-09-08_ag_M0010140.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seaweed","wikidata":"Q237169"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Ascophyllum_nodosum.jpg/500px-Ascophyllum_nodosum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Ascophyllum_nodosum.jpg/500px-Ascophyllum_nodosum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ascophyllum nodosum exposed to the sun in Nova Scotia, Canada","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAscophyllum_nodosum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Codiumfragile.jpg/500px-Cod